fact WorkItem(id: "WI-1012", description: some(value: "Refuse a supplier TIE at LOAD when the carrier is statically concrete, instead of only at the call. WI-1010 made a SECOND supplier of a DEFAULTED spec op loud (058 S4.9) rather than letting route order decide silently, and raised it at eval as 'EvalError::AmbiguousSpecOpDispatch' - the only wording that can name a route with no name of its own (an instance fact, S4.3). But the TYPER already holds everything the diagnostic needs at the moment it declines to pin: 'check_apply_iter' has the span, the carrier symbol and the rendered candidate list from 'SpecOpSupplier::render'. Deferring costs three things. (1) 'anthill check' passes on a program the interpreter will refuse - the repo principle is 'avoid fallbacks, know about errors early'. (2) A tie in a branch that never executes never reports. (3) WORST, and the reason this is filed rather than left: on the SLD path the refusal DEGRADES TO SILENCE - resolve.rs residualizes 'AmbiguousSpecOpDispatch' to None, so a rule body calling a 2-supplier defaulted op now answers NOTHING where pre-WI-1010 it answered the default. That is inherited family policy, documented in docs/kernel-language.md S'Where the ambiguity error is raised', and unreachable in the corpus today (WI-1010 measured the tie corpus-inert) - but it is exactly the 'prefer a loud error over a silent skip' case. WHY IT WAS NOT DONE INLINE: it needs a NEW 'TypeError' variant carrying (carrier: Symbol, candidates: Vec<String>, span). 'DispatchAmbiguous' cannot be reused - VERIFIED: its 'InstanceTie' carries PROVIDER symbols, and for a route-1-vs-route-2 tie both canonicalize to the SAME carrier, so 'render_instance_tie' would print 'Leaf, Leaf' and, both being concrete, pick 'TieRepair::ValueDirected', whose message ('each is a CONCRETE provider') does not describe this tie at all. WORK: add the variant + its 'format' / 'to_load_error' / 'span' arms, raise it from the typer's else-arm, and SHARE ONE MESSAGE BODY with the eval error (precedent: 'unselected_instance_message' already shares one sentence across 'TypeError::format' and two 'LoadError' renderings). The eval site STAYS - the typer's WI-444 block only fires on a statically concrete carrier, so an un-pinnable one still needs the late refusal; that is an argument for sharing the message, not for having only the late one. ACCEPTANCE: WI-1010's 'an_own_member_rivalled_by_a_fact_binding_is_refused_at_the_call' becomes a LOAD refusal (its concrete-'Leaf' program) while an abstract-carrier twin still refuses at eval, both quoting the same sentence; a rule-body case that today answers nothing reports something; full workspace green. REFERENCE: WI-1010; WI-842; WI-843; docs/design/058-implementation.md S12."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-1010"]), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "user", at: "2026-08-06T09:37:33Z"))

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-1012", author: "user", content: "IMPLEMENTED, workspace green 4212/0 (56 binaries); /code-review NOT YET RUN - it is user-invocable only. /simplify run instead (4 agents), findings applied.\n\nWHAT LANDED: new 'TypeError::AmbiguousSpecOpDispatch' + 'LoadError::AmbiguousSpecOpDispatch' (same name as the eval variant - one refusal, three faces, the 'MacroRejected' precedent), raised from the typer's WI-444 block when 'carrier_override_suppliers' returns >=2 on a statically concrete carrier. One message owner 'ambiguous_spec_op_dispatch_message' read by TypeError::format, both LoadError renderings and EvalError::Display. Ticket's acceptance met: WI-1010's tie test is now a LOAD refusal, the abstract-carrier twin still refuses at eval, and both render ONE body (asserted by byte-equality after stripping the load face's line:col).\n\nTHE REVIEW OVERTURNED MY MESSAGE, and it was the one thing I had already doubted. My shared sentence said 'No bracket at this site can choose between them' for EVERY tie. FALSE for the only tie shape the corpus drives: 'callee_requirement_slots' pushes a Dispatch slot only when 'lookup_spec_op_dispatch' is Some, so a BODY-LESS op's witness rival IS nameable via '[Spec = W]' - which is exactly WI-842's fixture (own member vs witness Rival). I had deleted WI-842's correct advice ('route the call through an operation that can write one'). The tree had said so twice: TieRepair's doc ('advertising a repair that the next compile rejects is the failure mode this enum exists to make impossible to reintroduce') and LoadError::CallTypeArgsNotSupportedHere's 'position' field, added because one wording told a rule head to route through an operation. I made the mirror-image error. FIX: 'SupplierTieRepair' {KeepOne, NameableWitness}, one owner 'supplier_tie_repair' asked by all three sites. Both arms driven - and WI-842's tie test had NO assertion on its message at all (it checked three names, all of which survived the wrong wording), so it now pins the repair and the sentence.\n\nTWO LIMITS, MEASURED, both filed rather than claimed closed:\n- WI-1026: a rule body naming the spec op DIRECTLY reaches none of this. Measured, 3 spellings x 2 supply shapes: with ONE fact-route supplier 'Desc.describe(leaf(), ?r)'=1 (the DEFAULT - so WI-1010's rule does not hold there either), 'describe(leaf(), ?r)'=[], 'leaf().describe(?r)'=1; with TWO: 1, [], 7. Three silent answers for one ambiguous program. CONTROL: the same tie through an operation body answers 7 / is refused at load, so the defect is the PATH. Pre-existing - neither WI-444 site is on the SLD goal path.\n- WI-1027: the BODY-LESS half has no load face; three declining arms of check_apply_iter hand a concrete carrier to eval unrefused. Structural reading from a review agent, NOT driven, so filed fixture-first. docs/kernel-language.md narrowed accordingly - the load refusal covers DEFAULTED operations, not the family. I had over-claimed it for both halves.\nAlso recorded AT the arm: it cannot fire for a SELF-RECEIVER spec (provision_carrier_sort files under the spec's FIRST TYPE PARAM), so the stdlib's largest defaulted-op family is out of reach - WI-450's limit, so closing it there stays a decision.\n\nBACK-OUT MATRICES RE-MEASURED, not carried over. New file: 5 of 6 rows FAIL when the '[_, _, ..]' arm falls through; the abstract-carrier row passes by design. WI-1010's tie row MOVED - was 'typer FAILS | eval FAILS | both FAILS', now 'typer FAILS | eval ok | both FAILS', because reverting the eval half alone leaves the typer to refuse. Measured over all four runs.\n\nALSO FROM REVIEW: 'render_suppliers' extracted - the typer made a THIRD copy of the candidate-rendering expression, which eval's 'sole_supplier_by_value' doc claims to exist to prevent; that doc was stale and is now corrected rather than left. wi1010's 'program' is 'pub(crate)' with a 'tail' param and shared (not copied), and the two duplicate control tests were deleted in favour of wi1010's, which fail identically if this arm over-fires. The rule test was renamed to 'a_rule_reaching_the_tie_through_an_operation_is_refused' and says at its site what it does NOT drive - review was right that it would pass with rule-body typing deleted.", at: "2026-08-06T09:18:57Z")
